AB  - A revolutionary method for collaborative health-care analytics, particularly about time series data, is federated learning (FL). Recent studies show that FL can provide strong privacy safeguards while maintaining high model usefulness in healthcare applications, especially when paired with privacy-enhancing technologies like homomorphic encryption, differential privacy, and secure multi-party computation (SMPC). Based on a large collection of foundation research, technologies, and applications, this study incorporates the most recent developments, real-world applications, and current issues in safe and privacy-centered federated learning (FL) for healthcare time-series data. A comprehensive literature search was conducted across over 170 million research papers in Consensus, encompassing databases such as Semantic Scholar, PubMed, and others. A total of 982 papers met the search strategy. Eight unique search groups were executed, focusing on privacy-preserving FL systems for healthcare. The surveyed literature shows that FL systems remain vulnerable to state-of-the-art attacks, including membership inference and model inversion, when the adversaries possess auxiliary information or when model upgrades are not adequately obfuscated. Despite significant progress, areas left uncovered by research are fairness, personalization, and communication efficiency. Understanding these trade-offs is essential for practical, scalable, and sustainable adoption in resource-constrained healthcare settings.
ER  - 

