AB  - This work focuses on the need for structured frameworks in sharing genomic data through ethical debates, attack methods and emerging solutions. Genomic datasets are sensitive and therefore issue of privacy is a concern because they are extensively applied in the current day medicine, research and consumer services. These databases can be attacked in a de- anonymization attack even without the identifiers attached since they can re-identify the person. Long-range familial searches reveal identities, which brings the question of consent as well as family implications and equity in genomic research. genomic beacons are query systems that can be attacked by allele inference attacks to alter the genotype of an individual. Observable characteristics can be traced to genomic records that can undermine its anonymization and re-identify researched people. The privacy issues involve surveying the systemic risks of sharing the genomic datasets and searching the research opportunities in case of safe cooperation. The problems associated with a synthetic dataset include keeping the accuracy but still provide perfect means of balancing between utility and privacy. All these issues can be addressed with the help of the multi key homomorphic encryption, which allows privacy preserving genomic calculations with the help of a state-of-the-art approach.
