AB  - The rise of Internet of Things (IoT) devices has brought the possibility to be connected higher than ever before, and increased the attack surface of cyber threats. Conventional centralized intrusion detection system/ (IDS) is limited by: Scalability overcapacity Data privacy Flexibility to heterogeneous IoT. In order to realize secure and cooperative intrusion detection over IoTs, the paper shall presuppose a system of federated learning (FL) to maintain patient privacy. On the proposed system, there are a few IoT clients that will be trained on-device on local deep learning models, encrypted with CKKS scheme under TenSEAL library, and sent via a central server to be aggregated safely without exposing raw data. The framework provides a chance to simulate settings of multiple clients, aggregate and encrypt fields of study, and test it on benchmark IoT datasets. Categorizing traffic as a normal or an attack is performed by a lightweight feedforward neural network, but the interface based on Streamlit assists the preprocessing of the data, federated training control, and real-time prediction. The experimental tests confirm that aggregation of the models can be performed with minimal performance degradation, compared to non-encrypted FL. In this manner, the privacy requirements are fulfilled, the risks of data leak are mitigated and it is the main part of the next-generation solution of IoT security that can be equally scaled.
