AB  - Federated learning (FL), a novel distributed machine learning technique proposed recently, is attractive for collaboratively training a model without sharing the raw data in plaintext while being important for the increasingly urgent demand for privacy, security and regulation. Although FL mitigates concerns regarding centralized data collection, it remains vulnerable to privacy exposure and adversarial attacks through the sharing of model updates. Therefore, designing the privacy-preserving and secure federated learning systems is an important research topic. In this paper, we conduct a thorough and systematic survey of privacy-preserving federated learning, where the basic definitions, threat assumptions, and defence approaches are discussed in detail. We begin by discussing the fundamentals, architectures, and taxonomies of FLs and subsequently cover privacy threats and security attacks, such as gradient leakage, inference privacy attacks, data/model poisoning attacks, backdoor attacks and Byzantine failure. We also review advanced privacy-preserving technologies, such as differential privacy and secure multiparty computation (SMPC)/fully homomorphic encryption (FHE)/secure aggregation, and discuss the trade-off between that ensures the privacy of the learner, model utility, computational overhead, and scalability. We also investigate how to robustly aggregate the ensemble and defend against the adversaries to improve its resilience against adversarial examples. This survey examines the use of privacy-preserving federated learning is used in healthcare, finance, IoT, mobile computing, and collaboration between organizations. It also highlights some challenges and future research areas, such as handling non-IID data, balancing privacy and usefulness, scalability, fairness, incentives, and new privacy technologies. In summary, this survey aims to be a helpful guide for those who want to create secure and reliable federated learning systems..
ER  - 

