AB  - This paper proposes a Hierarchical PrivacyPreserving Federated Learning (HPP-FL) scheme that integrates Differential Privacy (DP) and Homomorphic Encryption (HE) to enhance privacy protection for EEG data in IoT-based healthcare scenarios. To address risks of intermediate feature leakage and decision parameter exposure in conventional federated learning, the proposed scheme introduces a two-phase privacy protection mechanism. First, dynamic Gaussian noise perturbation with dynamic privacy budget control is applied exclusively to the client-side feature extraction layers to defend against model inversion attacks. Second, a gradient magnitude ranking strategy identifies 60 % of high-sensitivity parameters in classification head, which are then encrypted using Paillier homomorphic encryption to mitigate parameter inference attacks. By establishing a hierarchical parameter processing protocol, where the server performs standard federated averaging on plaintext feature parameters while conducting homomorphic aggregation on encrypted classification parameters, the proposed scheme strikes an effective balance between privacy protection and model performance. Simulation results on the BCI Competition IV 2a motor imagery dataset demonstrate that the proposed HPPFL effectively enhances raw data privacy against leakage while maintaining test accuracy comparable to baseline approaches, demonstrating its practicality and effectiveness for secure medical federated learning applications.
ER  - 

