AB  - This study introduces SEC-EHRSAF, a secure cloud-based Electronic Health Record (EHR) storage framework built on the Boneh-Goh-Nissim (BGN) cryptosystem. The investigation tackles data security issues for public healthcare cloud storage by studying India’s Primary Health Centres (PHCs) and Government Hospitals (GHs). SEC-EHRSAF operates in three modes: secure storage of EHRs, controlled data transfer, and privacy-preserving analytics. The system uses Partial Homomorphic Encryption (PHE) as its storage security method alongside re-encryption schemes to facilitate data sharing operations. Performance is optimized using Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) and Baby Step-Giant Step (BSGS) algorithms, achieving an execution time of 5.1 ms at 80-bit security—faster than existing schemes. The research proves how cloud computing supports secure Electronic Health Record (EHR) storage alongside fast access and predictive analysis functions. Practical applicability was validated through experiments on Amazon Web Services (AWS) using both mobile and desktop platforms. The framework ensures end-to-end healthcare data protection, enabling secure sharing and analytics that strengthen public health decision-making.
