AB  - Federated Learning (FL) is an application that allows training models collaboratively, retaining the privacy of clients, but being extremely susceptible to attackers that poison data, especially in healthcare settings with heterogeneous and sensitive data. Such attacks impair the model integrity and lower the predictive performance and jeopardize patient safety. In order to solve this dilemma, we introduce an innovative adaptive defense model, which integrates anomaly recognition, secure aggregation, and powerful filtering of clients to detect and avoid malicious updates in the training process. The algorithm also uses homomorphic encryption and similarity-based clustering to identify forged or poisonous gradient updates with uncompromising privacy conditions. Wide-ranging experiments on real-world medical data proves the fact that the given method is highly effective to counter adversarial attacks, with the final global model accuracy reaching 99.96, surpassing the solutions currently available, including Trimmed-Mean and Bulyan. The present paper is a contribution to a scalable, secure, and privacy-preserving answer to the resilient FL in the healthcare application.
