AB  - Federated learning enables multiple parties to collaboratively train machine learning models without sharing raw data, but it still risks privacy leakage through exposed model updates and often incurs heavy communication costs. In this work, we present a practical and privacy-preserving federated learning framework that integrates homomorphic encryption—specifically the CKKS scheme with proxy re-encryption—to ensure that model updates remain encrypted throughout the training process. Our system allows clients to locally train models, encrypt their updates, and send them to a central server, which performs secure aggregation without ever accessing plaintext parameters. To validate deployability, we implement support for metadata management, communication tracking, and round-wise timing analysis. Experiments on an LSTM-based sequence model and a real-world financial fraud detection dataset demonstrate that our approach achieves accuracy comparable to standard FL, while offering strong protection against parameter leakage and inference attacks. Furthermore, we provide detailed measurements of communication overhead and runtime, highlighting the trade-offs between privacy, efficiency, and accuracy. By addressing both security and scalability, this framework advances the feasibility of deploying federated learning in sensitive, multi-party domains such as healthcare and finance.
