AB  - The proliferation of machine learning (ML) in sensitive domains such as healthcare is severely hindered by increasing concerns over data privacy and stringent regulations. While Privacy-Preserving Machine Learning (PPML) techniques, particularly Homomorphic Encryption (HE), offer rigorous theoretical guarantees against data leakage, they frequently do so at the cost of degradation in model accuracy. This research proposes a novel system utilizing post-quantum cryptography and ensemble learning to mitigate accuracy loss and reduce the rate of false negatives. To achieve this, disjoint base learners are trained under strict HE guarantees and secure aggregation mechanisms are developed with the unique aim of reducing false negative predictions. The final system is a robust PPML framework that demonstrates state-of-the-art accuracy at $91 \%$ compared to existing single-model approaches while simultaneously reducing false negatives by an average of $66 \%$. Additionally, the proposed system is capable of analyzing 7,500 images per hour, representing a $55 \%$ increase in performance.
ER  - 

