AB  - With the increase in distributed and privacy-sensitive data generated from healthcare, finance, and IoT sectors, it becomes necessary to enable cooperative analysis among organizations without aggregating sensitive data in a centralized manner. Federated Learning can meet this demand through enabling training of the model with the data kept local at each client. Recently, however, it has been shown that the exchange of plaintext model parameters is still susceptible to gradient inversion and membership inference attacks. To protect against such attacks, we propose a federated learning architecture that utilizes differential privacy with adaptive noise schedule, CKKS based homomorphic encryption, and secure aggregation. Contrary to the current literature that only examines each component independently, the proposed framework not only unifies all those methods into one but also formally guarantees $\varepsilon$-differential privacy composition for training as a whole. Specifically, our adaptive noise scheduling algorithm allows us to decrease the amount of Laplace noise during each federated round in order to ensure faster convergence while maintaining overall privacy budget. The experimental evaluations conducted on MNIST and UCI HAR dataset with non-IID federated partition show consistent stability during the training phase and up to 91.9% accuracy while the encryption overhead remains below 5%.
ER  - 

