AB  - In recent years, privacy-preserving medical image segmentation has become vital for confirming safety and dependability of multi-institutional collaborations. However, traditional models often fail maintain segmentation accuracy owing to noise injection and restricted parameter exchange. Hence, this research proposes a novel framework called secure aggregation and homomorphic entropy-based federated learning (SAHE-FL) for Brain Tumor (BT) segmentation. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) scans were collected from Brain Tumor Segmentation (BraTS)2020 dataset. The collected data were then preprocessed using intensity normalization, bounding-box cropping, and uniform volume resizing, followed by widespread data augmentation to improve generalization. Subsequently, the processed data were fed into 3Dimensional Fully Convolutional Neural Network (3D U-Net) architecture to extract both local tumor boundaries and global contextual features from various MRI modalities. In addition, SAHE-FL incorporates secure aggregation with homomorphic encryption, which confirms client model updates remain encrypted and masked throughout training, thereby removing need for artificial noise while preserving accuracy. Finally, a secure aggregation mechanism combined with homomorphic encryption safeguards that only aggregated updates was revealed providing reliable privacy protection without reducing model accuracy. Experimental results show that proposed SAHE-FL model attained superior results in terms of sensitivity 0.90% than existing Differential Privacy and Partial Weight Sharing-based Federated Learning (DP-PWS-FL) model.
ER  - 

